  "summary": "Traditional Japanese room with tatami mat flooring designed for multifunctional living.",
  "description": "Features tatami mats, sliding fusuma doors, and recessed tokonoma alcoves. Developed to accommodate flexible living arrangements in small spaces while reflecting Japanese concepts of simplicity and harmony with nature.",
  "culturalContext": "The washitsu — traditional Japanese room — is tatami-mat proportion made spatial law: 4.5- and 6-mat rooms for tea, shoji screens filtering light, tokonoma alcove displays (hanging scroll, seasonal ikebana), and furniture-free flexibility (futon out by night). Modern Japan keeps one washitsu in many homes; hotels, tea houses and museums preserve the grammar, and \"tatami proportion\" remains the base module of Japanese spatial aesthetics.",
